        ":question/id": "15aecd0c-7a63-5ed0-a8d5-0fcdbef27db3",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 2 \\left(e^{2 x} + 2\\right)^{2} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 2>0$.\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=e^{2 x} + 2$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 2>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2 \\left(e^{2 x} + 2\\right)^{2} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int u^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int u^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{u^{3}}{3}+C.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\int u^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{u^{3}}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\left(e^{2 x} + 2\\right)^{3}}{3}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
